<p>Late at night, on a boat heading up the Nile toward Qina, a Moroccan diplomat sat in his cabin studying by candlelight. He was thirty-four years old, he had been on the road for a year, and he was carrying books, papers and pens the way scholars of his tradition always did - the <em>rihla</em>, the travel account, was a genre you wrote while moving.</p>

<p>The ship was taken by Christian corsairs. The books went into the hands of men who could not read them. The diplomat went to the island of Rhodes, then to a cell in Castel Sant'Angelo in Rome, and then - in a turn that still reads as implausible - into the Vatican library, where within a few years he would be correcting the Pope's Arabic manuscripts and writing, in a language he had barely begun at thirty, the book that Europe used as its map of Africa for the next three hundred years.</p>

<p>His name was al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Wazzan al-Fasi al-Gharnati. History remembers him as Leo Africanus.</p>

<div class="not-prose my-8 rounded-2xl border border-primary/20 bg-primary/5 p-6">
<p class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-primary mb-2">The facts at a glance</p>
<ul class="space-y-1.5 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
<li><strong>Born:</strong> about 1494, Granada, in the last years of Nasrid rule</li>
<li><strong>Raised:</strong> Fez, Morocco, after his family left Spain</li>
