<h2>Granada, and the Year Everything Closed</h2>

<p>He was born into a family of clerics and court accountants serving the Nasrid dynasty - the family of the <em>muhtasib</em>, the magistrate who inspected markets, weights and morals. His father Ahmad served in the court of Muhammad XII, the ruler Europeans called Boabdil. The household spoke Arabic; the streets spoke Spanish. He learned early that a city can require two languages of you before you have decided to learn anything.</p>

<p>In 1492 Granada surrendered, and the terms that were supposed to protect the Muslim population did not hold for long. His family left - whether just before or just after the surrender is not certain - and crossed to Fez, where an uncle was already established as a diplomat to the Wattasid court. That connection bought them a house in a good quarter. Many other Granadan refugee families were not so lucky and said so publicly.</p>

<p>This is the first thing worth noticing about his education, because it is the thing that would later look like luck and was in fact leverage: the family arrived with a skill that a court wanted. His uncle's position did not make al-Hasan a scholar, but it put him inside the system that produced scholars.</p>

<h2>Al-Qarawiyyin, and the Hospital That Paid for It</h2>

<p>He began, like most boys in the city, at a neighbourhood school learning to read and write Arabic and to memorise the Quran. Memorising the whole text is a specific kind of training: you learn to hold large quantities of language you do not yet fully understand, and to retrieve it on cue, in order, without a book. He then moved to the madrasa and to al-Qarawiyyin - the mosque-university founded in the ninth century by Fatima al-Fihri, and one of the oldest continuously operating centres of learning anywhere.</p>

<p>The curriculum was Arabic grammar and rhetoric, with poetry at the centre of it, plus religious doctrine and Islamic jurisprudence. Poetry mattered more than it sounds. In that tradition, a man who could quote verse appropriately in an argument was demonstrating not decoration but command - a public, memorised corpus, deployed in real time, judged by listeners who knew it as well as he did.</p>

<p>He was not a wealthy student. To pay his fees he worked for two years as a notary at the hospital in Fez, drafting and witnessing documents. This is the part of his biography that tends to get skipped in favour of the sea voyage, and it is the part that explains the book. A notary spends his days on contracts, inventories, deeds, bills of sale and estate records: the exact vocabulary of property, money and obligation that a geographical description of a continent turns out to need. <strong>He was employed, at twenty, in the precise register that would make him useful at fifty.</strong></p>

<h2>Timbuktu, and the Books Sold for More Than Gold</h2>

<p>Around 1510 he travelled with his uncle on a diplomatic mission to the Songhai Empire, reaching Gao and Timbuktu. It was a working trip, not a Grand Tour: the purpose was to build ties with rulers whose gold and salt trade mattered to Fez.</p>

<p>What he saw there he later described in the passage that made Timbuktu famous in Europe, and that is quoted in almost every account of the city's manuscripts:</p>

<blockquote>
<p>"Hither are brought diverse manuscripts or written books out of Barbarie, which are sold for more money than any other merchandise."</p>
</blockquote>

<p>He had arrived in a city where the book trade was a luxury trade, where the Djinguereber mosque held a library, and where scholarship was a form of wealth. The observation is not a traveller's wonder at the exotic. It is a notary and a graduate of al-Qarawiyyin recognising a familiar economy in an unfamiliar place - which is, in miniature, how he would later write the whole continent.</p>

<p>After that mission he travelled on his own account, representing the Sultan of Fez and other rulers: through North Africa, into Egypt, to Constantinople, to Portugal. By thirty he had a working knowledge of the political map of the western Mediterranean and a professional habit of writing things down.</p>

<h2>Rhodes, Castel Sant'Angelo, and a Pope Who Collected Scholars</h2>

<p>In the summer of 1518 he boarded a ship from Cairo to return to Fez. Spanish corsairs took it - near Djerba, or more probably near Crete. The normal fate of an unransomed Muslim captive was the galley bench. What saved him was the cargo: his captors understood that they were holding a man who knew things.</p>

<p>He was held on Rhodes, the base of the Knights Hospitaller, then moved to Rome and presented to Pope Leo X, who had the family habit of collecting remarkable people. Leo lodged him in Castel Sant'Angelo - not in the dungeons, which is where the Pope's enemies went, but in the apartments with access to the library. In 1519, still technically a prisoner, al-Hasan signed his name in a manuscript he had borrowed from the Vatican collection.</p>

<p>The Pope's offer was straightforward and not really an offer: freedom, a pension and a scholarly career, on condition of baptism. Al-Hasan was a diplomat, he understood exactly how much room he had, and he took it. Two bishops were assigned to instruct him - Paride Grassi, master of papal ceremonies, and Giovanni Battista Bonciani, the Pope's former tutor. On the Feast of Epiphany, 6 January 1520, he was baptised in St Peter's as Johannes Leo de Medicis: John Lion, of the Medici. In Arabic he rendered it <em>Yuhanna al-Asad al-Gharnati</em> - John the Lion of Granada. The Granada he kept. The Medici he took from his patron.</p>

<p>Grassi kept a diary, and one entry describes what the papal court actually thought of its new convert:</p>

<blockquote>
<p>"Truly learned ... for in his language he is said to be most expert in philosophy and medicine, on which count, many philosophers and physicians came to dispute with him. And to universal praise, he corrected manuscripts in the Arabic language, which in many places were falsely, foolishly and badly interpreted."</p>
</blockquote>

<h2>How He Actually Learned Italian</h2>

<p>Here is the part that is usually told as an adventure, and is more interesting as a method.</p>

<p>He arrived in Italy at about twenty-four with Arabic, some spoken Spanish from a Granada childhood, and the liturgical and scholarly Arabic of a Fez education. He did not arrive with Italian or Latin. Within six years he had written a long geographical treatise in Italian, compiled thousands of dictionary entries, translated the Epistles of Paul into Arabic for an Italian prince, and corrected a Latin-Arabic Quran translation. Strip out the drama and what is left is a very specific language-learning programme, and it was not self-study.</p>

<h3>He was immersed in the written register, not the spoken one</h3>

<p>His classroom was the Vatican library. That is not a metaphor: he had access to manuscripts, and he used it. Reading in a language you are acquiring gives you the high register first - the formal, the technical, the written. This is the opposite of how most learners today are told to proceed, and it worked here for a reason: the work he was being paid to do was written work.</p>

<h3>He learned by correcting, which is the hardest reading there is</h3>

<p>His first documented Italian job was textual criticism of Arabic manuscripts for the papal court. Correcting a text forces a level of attention that reading for comprehension never reaches: you have to decide, line by line, whether the words in front of you are right, and you cannot skip the parts you do not understand. Grassi's note that physicians and philosophers came to dispute with him says the same thing from the other side - he was being tested orally, in public, by specialists, and passing.</p>

<h3>He built a dictionary, which is the fastest way to learn a lexicon</h3>

<p>In Bologna in 1523 he met Jacob Mantino, a Jewish physician and translator, and the two of them compiled an Arabic-Hebrew-Latin medical vocabulary. The division of labour is the interesting part: <strong>Leo contributed 5,500 Arabic entries. Mantino contributed 170 Hebrew entries and 230 Latin entries.</strong></p>

<p>Read that ratio carefully. He was the native informant, supplying the Arabic side, which means that for every one of those 5,500 entries he had to produce the Italian or Latin equivalent, or approve one. That is a vocabulary course of enormous size, conducted at the level of precision where a synonym is not good enough - in medical terminology, where the wrong word is a wrong diagnosis. Nobody designed it as a learning exercise. It did the job anyway.</p>

<h3>He taught, which is the oldest accelerator there is</h3>

<p>By 1525 he was living at the household of Cardinal Egidio da Viterbo as the cardinal's Arabic tutor. In 1532, after he had left Italy, the orientalist Johann Widmannstetter was still being directed to him in Tunis as the man to see if you wanted to learn Arabic. A tutor cannot fake it. Explaining a grammar to an adult who is paying for it finds every gap in your own understanding within a week.</p>

<h2>The Book, and the Habit That Made It Possible</h2>

<p>Leo X died in 1521, and the new pope, Adrian VI, was suspicious of a Muslim at court. Leo left Rome and spent three or four years moving through Italy - Bologna, Viterbo - before returning in 1526 under Clement VII, another Medici, who resumed the patronage. In that year he finished the manuscript he had presumably been working on for years: <em>Libro de la Cosmographia et Geographia de Africa</em>.</p>

<p>It is not a travel diary. It is nine books: a general account of climate, culture, economy, geography and government, then seven regional surveys of deserts, mountains, towns and peoples, and a final book on animals, plants, minerals and rivers. He wrote it in Italian, a language he had been using for less than a decade, and he wrote it with no maps - a striking omission for a work of geography, and one that makes the text's dependence on memory and description even more obvious.</p>

<p>Its afterlife is the measure of the thing. Giovanni Battista Ramusio published it in Venice in 1550 as <em>Descrittione dell'Africa</em>. It was reprinted five times. Latin and French editions appeared in 1556; John Pory's English translation, <em>A Geographical Historie of Africa</em>, in 1600 - and Pory's English was made from the Latin, which was itself full of errors, which means the version Shakespeare may have read was twice removed from what Leo wrote. It remained Europe's standard authority on the continent until European exploration in the nineteenth century replaced description with survey.</p>

<p>That legacy is double-edged, and it should be said plainly. The book gave Europe its imaginative geography of Africa - the kingdoms, the trade routes, the salt and gold, the learning of Timbuktu - and it also gave later European expansion a convenient inventory. A description made by an insider for a patron became, three centuries later, a reference for people with very different intentions.</p>

<h2>The Last Years</h2>

<p>In 1527 imperial troops sacked Rome. Leo left, and there is no certain record of the route. By 1532 he was in Tunis, where Widmannstetter hoped to meet him and reported that he had returned to Islam. Whether he reconverted, or whether that was what a German orientalist in Italy was told, is not resolvable from the surviving evidence. He published nothing after 1526. He died around 1554, probably in Tunis, about sixty years old.</p>

<h2>What His Method Was</h2>

<p>Sort the biography into its mechanisms and a pattern appears that has nothing to do with genius and everything to do with design.</p>

<p><strong>He learned the language from inside a job that required it.</strong> Not a course, not a tutor's hour: a post, with tasks, deadlines and consequences. The manuscripts had to be corrected; the dictionary entries had to be right; the cardinal's lesson had to be useful. Every hour of acquisition was attached to an outcome.</p>

<p><strong>He learned the written register first, because the written register was what he was hired for.</strong> Learners are often told to prioritise speech. He is the counterexample: if your life in the language will be documents, you should learn from documents, however badly you speak at first.</p>

<p><strong>He used the dictionary as a two-way drill rather than a reference.</strong> Compiling 5,500 entries is the most thorough vocabulary exercise ever accidentally assigned to a language learner. He could not look at a word without producing its equivalent and checking it against a physician who would notice.</p>

<p><strong>He had something to say before he had the words to say it.</strong> This is the least imitable and most important part. He knew the geography of North and West Africa from having ridden across it, and he knew what a court wanted to hear about it. The language was the wrapper, not the content. When he sat down in 1526 to write nine books of description, he was not searching for material and hoping the Italian would carry it. He was translating a lifetime of material into a language he had learned by translating.</p>

<blockquote>
<p>A language learned with nothing to say is a subject. A language learned because you are the only person in the room who knows the answer is a tool - and tools get used, which is how they get sharp.</p>
</blockquote>

<h2>What to Take From Him</h2>

<p>Three things, and none of them is "be a genius".</p>

<p>First, find the job before the course. If you can be the person who does something in the language - correct a translation, staff a desk, write summaries of documents for someone who cannot read them - the language will follow the work. This is the same mechanism that makes <a href="/stories/omar-ibn-said-arabic-autobiography/">Omar ibn Said's Arabic survive decades of deliberate erasure</a>: it was the language in which he thought and prayed, not a subject he had studied.</p>

<p>Second, do not despise the dictionary. Compiling entries, even badly, even for yourself, is retrieval practice with a quality check attached, and it is the closest a solitary learner can get to what a paying client does for you.</p>

<p>Third, and hardest: build something in the language before you feel ready. Leo Africanus wrote Europe's Africa in a language he had started at twenty-four, in a city where he was a convert, a captive and a curiosity. It was not fluent prose by the standards of a Florentine humanist, and it was still the best book on the subject for three hundred years. Competence is not a door you pass through before you are allowed to begin. It is what happens to a person who began anyway.</p>

